module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.dissolution_trigger_cites_a_mechanism_that_is_later_deleted

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BlockerClassUnstated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data dissolution_trigger_cites_a_mechanism_that_is_later_deleted: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "dissolution_trigger_cites_a_mechanism_that_is_later_deleted" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: BlockerClassUnstated },

  receipts: [
    "**a dissolution trigger cites a MECHANISM by name, that mechanism is later deleted, and nothing points back -- so the scaffold it governs becomes permanent while still reading as tracked** (INVALID STATE: a declared scaffold or rung drop names its exit as a mechanism -- a lens, a gate, a cadence, a flag -- and the citation is CORRECT WHEN WRITTEN. A later lane deletes that mechanism for its own good reasons, and the deletion is complete and reviewed at ITS end. The trigger is not a consumer of the deleted symbol -- it is prose naming it -- so no compile refuses, no census counts it, and the scaffold now carries an exit condition that can never occur. HARM: the row keeps presenting as temporary debt with a named exit, which is exactly the presentation that removes it from every audit that looks for stalls; gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder (no untracked stall)'s separation of `cannot climb further` from `can climb after one grounding` silently reclassifies the row into the first category without anyone deciding that. DISTINGUISHING FACTS, and they are what make this its own class rather than an instance of the two neighbours: (1) THE TENSE AND THE DIRECTION OF INVALIDATION. `restoration_promise_names_a_route_that_does_not_exist` is a promise whose named route NEVER EXISTED, discoverable by grepping the tree at the moment the promise is read; `trigger_satisfied_before_the_row_was_written` is a trigger already TRUE at authoring. Here the trigger was both real and unsatisfied when written, and a THIRD PARTY's edit made it unreachable afterwards, so neither of those rows' origin arms applies and neither of their remedies finds it. (2) THE DELETING LANE IS BLAMELESS AND CANNOT SEE IT. A mechanism's deletion PR reviews the mechanism's consumers -- imports, calls, roster membership -- and a trigger citing it is none of those, so the class is invisible from the only side that knows the deletion is happening. This is gunbc.design_argument::single_authority's positional-citation defect moved from `file:line` to `mechanism-name`: a citation that decays because the thing it names is edited elsewhere. SPECIMEN, MEASURED 2026-09-05 AT HEAD 4da9c1a3fdd AND UNREPAIRED AT FILING: `v2.compiler.self_host.candidate_generation` carries `SCAFFOLD: feature:medium_structure_containment` over `direct_rust_door_cargo_toml`, the hand-authored Cargo manifest literal the direct Rust door writes. The cited lens WAS DELETED IN #6831, stated by two independent surviving carriers -- `v2.std.compilers.target_model` `target_text_carrier_scaffold_note` (`The retired no-smuggled-programs / medium_structure_containment lens (#6831) is not live enforcement`) and `gunbc.plans.self_applying_lenses` (`medium_structure_containment / no-smuggled-programs was deleted in #6831`). The name occurs three times in the corpus and NOT ONCE as a live declaration: the annotation that cites it, and the two carriers recording its death. THE SECOND HALF OF THE SPECIMEN IS WHAT MAKES THE REPAIR NON-COSMETIC: the same annotation's `dissolve-on` SENTENCE names a different and real capability -- emit the manifest through the target-model / serialize_target path or a cited Toml medium carrier -- and THAT capability does not exist either, measured: there is no TOML authority anywhere under `src/v2/extdeps/formats` (json, sql, spice, icestorm_pcf) or `extdeps/languages`. So a reader who repairs only the dead NAME still holds a trigger nobody can satisfy; the honest repair states the capability and prices it as absent. RUNG FOUND AT: 1, mitigatable. Nothing silent is produced -- the scaffold is declared and its content is honest about being a literal -- and the only thing containing the harm is that a reader may notice the cited mechanism is gone. CEILING: 3, structurally guaranteed. A trigger that cites a repository symbol is a CITATION, and gunbc.design_argument::single_authority's standing rule already requires citations to name symbols the namespace authority can resolve; a trigger citing a symbol that no longer resolves is decidable by the same `Node`-tree read that decides any stale name, so an `Accepted` program need not contain one. NOT 4: the trigger is prose and a trigger naming a capability in words rather than a symbol is legitimate and must stay writable, so the invalid state remains authorable and the guarantee is a refusal rather than an absent constructor. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, at capability grain: dissolution and restoration triggers carrying their cited mechanism as a RESOLVED DECLARATION REFERENCE rather than as free text, so deleting the mechanism reddens every trigger that names it in the deleting lane's own build -- sufficient because the failure is precisely that the deleting lane cannot see the citation, and a convention that authors should check would be satisfied while the capability stayed dead. WHAT IS HARDER TO DO NOW THAN BEFORE THIS ROW WAS FILED: NOTHING, and this row is reported UNREMEDIED on that basis, per `a_written_row_is_not_a_firing_mechanism`. The one friction delivered with it is local and expires: the specimen's own trigger text is rewritten in the same change to name the absent capability instead of the deleted lens.)",

    "**SIGHTING, 2026-11-27 -- the deleted route surviving as the NAMED EXECUTOR of present-tense prose, not just as a named exit. Subject: claim_batch, measured by silent-swift-73 at commit 0bb8a83c593040c3b436eded3ab416bdbcc8e1b5. Route absence, re-measured by silent-swift-73 at cc526cad7ff063a990db2075ba5b48e814878678 with units and patterns, SUPERSEDING his earlier chunk-header absolutes (a src-scoped count stated as tree-wide, and an unqualified 'no ClaimBatch variant') -- corrected at source, not quietly improved: the claim_batch ARGV route is absent -- the token --claim-batch appears on 0 lines in src, no exact quoted argv token for it exists anywhere in src, the identifier ClaimBatch appears on 0 lines in src with 0 match arms and 0 occurrences in Rust variant or constructor position, and there is no claim-batch target among the 16 files in src/v1/stage0/src/bin, where claim_executor.rs exists and nothing named claim_batch does. What survives is the typed classification, not the route: ClaimBatch remains a live variant of type TestBatchRoute in gunbc.target_invocation (declared at :295 at the cited head, returned at :309, tested at :363 and :421, paired refusals ModeOptionOnNonClaimBatch at :351 and SourceRootsOnNonClaimBatch at :405) -- the declaration still stands on current main. Prose mentioning claim_batch does appear in src (149 lines across 64 files at cc526cad, of which 10 are quoted measured_by provenance strings in src/v2/workflow/floor_eval_step_cost_drop.dag, lines 187 through 360 as he lists them) -- provenance for numbers taken at runs of the deleted route, not an invocable path. The instructive contrast: the argv route died and the typed claim-batch concept did not, and the prose could not tell the difference. Yet dag lines still name it as what runs things: of 652 lines naming claim_batch at that head -- a total that reproduces exactly -- 51 are in present-tense execution form under his predicate, quoted verbatim so any reader can re-derive at their own commit: is executed|executed by|runs under|runs on|is run by|every run|each run|the batch runs|executes (case-insensitive), applied to dag lines naming claim_batch. MINUS the 4 dag/gunbc/ci/ci_layer_roots.dag lines, which were routed to Manager A, the distributed candidate list is 47 lines; my own dedup of the six delivered chunks prints 47 unique file:line keys across 37 files with zero duplicates (received-list sha256 afbd5ee94a6ae0b4349feb67be81540d46aaf0d836cef7e94aada4f2396dfeef over the line records). All numbers stated, delta 4 named to the CI exclusion, nothing silently reconciled. The earlier chunk-header companion classes (216 history-marked, 423 identifiers) are EXCLUDED from this row's citations: they do not reproduce under any predicate printed in the record, and 216+51+423 sums to 690 against the 652-line population -- overlapping by construction. Only reproducible numbers are carried: the 652 total, the 51-line present-tense class under the embedded predicate, and the 47-line list. Canonical list digest with its recipe beside it, not in a footnote (silent-swift-73, corrected 2026-11-27): sort the 47 citation records as file:line by file then ascending line number, join with LF, one trailing LF, sha256 over the UTF-8 bytes -- canonical_47_line_sha256 = 16f5a4a38cb50193e1d91652561e9b592ac86d4328a3650831cf3ac99c294234; my own received-list digest afbd5ee94a6ae0b4349feb67be81540d46aaf0d836cef7e94aada4f2396dfeef, computed by me over records I hold, stands without requiring trust in any sender. An earlier chunk-header digest (acf7a5b1...) is STRUCK from this row: its producing recipe was never recorded, and a digest without a recipe is a number that invites confidence and cannot be checked -- the same species of defect this row records. HEAD DRIFT, stated so a later reader is not misled: the 47-key list is bound to 0bb8a83c5930; at cc526cad itself only 44 of 47 still match -- dag/gunbc/design_ruling.dag:1119, dag/gunbc/design_ruling.dag:1768 and dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_authority.dag:8425 no longer carry the predicate. Drift at a later head is what head-binding looks like, not an error.",

    "WHY THIS ROW AND NOT THE NEIGHBOURS -- the discriminator is TENSE AND DIRECTION. gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::restoration_promise_names_a_route_that_does_not_exist is future-direction: the promise names a run, lane, cadence or sweep the tree DOES NOT CONTAIN AT WRITING TIME -- the route never existed. gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::receipt_subject_surface_outlives_its_own_production_time is about an admission gate pinned to a fast-moving digest, a wall that can never be satisfied while the gate itself refuses correctly -- neither construction matches. The claim_batch lines were TRUE WHEN WRITTEN: the route existed and really executed what they said. A later lane deleted the route completely and at its own end for its own good reasons -- and the prose survived intact, naming an executor that can no longer be invoked, with no compile-time consumer to force the edit. That is this row's constructor exactly: a citation CORRECT WHEN WRITTEN, a deletion complete and reviewed at ITS end, prose as the only consumer, and an assertion that now reads as tracked while being forever unfulfillable. The instance generalizes the row's wording from exit-mechanism to executor-mechanism (the cited route is what RUNS the work, not what dissolves the scaffold) -- same constructor, same silence, same repair: corrected when a change touches or relies on the line, citation of the deletion at the touching site.",

    "HANDLING -- SIGHTING ONLY, as instructed in the lane brief and as silent-swift-73 frames his own measurement -- the 47 lines are CANDIDATE LOCATIONS, NOT verdicts. The predicate catches execution FORM, not truth; some of the 47 are legitimate history sitting outside the marker vocabulary his 216-line history class grepped. There is no standalone sweep row -- a census of stale prose is the retired census machinery (bad_process_is_worse_than_no_process); a line is corrected when a change touches it or relies on it. Prior art pointing at this same owner: my own claim_batch self-audit marked the pre-route local_run*.sh lineage VOID at route time and labelled superseded run links superseded, rather than letting present-tense claims about an invocable route stand.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
